    {
      "id": "p1",
      "text": "Analysis of the May 2026 Visayas grid incident reveals a clear causal chain. The event initiating the chain of evidence was the tripping offline of a major power unit. This unscheduled outage, combined with pre-existing, documented maintenance gaps, exposed the grid to a measurable peak demand scenario driven by seasonal heat. The grid management entity, responding to the verified equipment failure, issued a red alert and initiated load shedding as an emergency measure to prevent a documented overload risk, resulting in a quantified supply deficit exceeding 950 megawatts.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Sage",
        "fragments": [
          "why-0",
          "why-1",
          "why-2",
          "how-0",
          "how-1",
          "how-2",
          "result-0"
        ],
        "channel_role": "toned"
      },
      "warnings": [],
      "fit": 0.88
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "text": "The sequence began with a confirmed mechanical failure at a critical power plant component. Decision-makers acting on evidence then placed the island group under a grid status change, a direct response to the resulting energy demand spike. The subsequent load shedding order was a necessary grid stabilization action. The incident serves as a case study in grid management under stress, where the loss of generation capacity can rapidly translate into a significant energy deficit.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Sage",
        "fragments": [
          "how-0",
          "how-1",
          "why-2",
          "how-2",
          "result-0"
        ],
        "channel_role": "toned"
      },
      "warnings": [],
      "fit": 0.85
    },
    {
      "id": "p3",
      "text": "Forensic review of the event timeline shows the triggering event occurred in early May. The system monitor's subsequent emergency response, while precautionary, highlights the vulnerability of energy infrastructure to sudden operational failures. The loss was not isolated; it reflected a broader pattern of plant outages that reduced system resilience. The final tally—a capacity gap of over 950 MW—underscores the tangible cost when multiple failures coincide during peak demand.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Sage",
